Q: How many Hackers are needed to switch a light bulb?

A: (Spoken with a Star Trek computer voice:)
Unable to determine the accurate numbers involved.
Explanation:
First, a Quest Leader is summoned to command the operation.
A priest is chosen to produce holy water to bless the bulb
to effectively decrease the chances of it exploding.
One of the Hackers tries, in vain, to search for the old light bulb. 
Another brings a blessed magic lamp to the scene, arguing that 
it's as good as a light bulb. "But", says the Quest Leader of Hackers,
"someone would surely steal the lamp and cash it for a wish, drain the lamp
of oil and polymorph it, leaving us nothing. It's too risky." Ensuing protests
are cut short with the Vorpal Blade. Another Hacker grabs the lamp and wishes
for a new bulb, but no such object exists in the game. After more failed
tries, the emerged djinni says "Screw it" and vanishes. 
Things start looking more hopeful when one individual starts coding
a new item into the game, a light bulb. After furious five minutes
of coding, the Coder releases a patch. "How do I add a patch 
to the game?", asks a Newbie. "Can I use a butcher's knife and
Terminator-bubble gum to do it? Will it work?" The rgrn goes on to
explain the basic principles of patching the game to the Newbie.
"Hey, why can't you release a binary?", asks one Hacker, not quite
a hacker. "Who says I can't?", asks the Coder. "It's just that I usually
don't. But ok, here it is." Binaries of the compiled game with the
nifty patch are released for almost every platform imaginable. 
After a day or two, one Hacker reports a bug ("Why aren't moths 
attracted to the light provided by the light bulb?"), and another one 
provides a possible solution. The Coder acts quickly.
However, the new patch breaks the save/bones compability, resulting
in a huge mob of Hackers vanishing into "a tree, a tree, a trickery!"

